/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  The declared property system.  It says WHICH components, WHICH formulation
  and WHICH models -- and nothing else.  Three things people expect to find
  here and which live elsewhere, each for the same reason (one home):

    * the CHEMISTRY (which equilibria exist) -- it comes with the components
      and the records in constant/chemistry/;
    * WHICH SOLID PHASES are admitted -- constant/chemistryDict;
    * the REFERENCE RUNG of each phase -- it is a consequence of the
      property method, not an independent choice.  Selecting the formulation
      settles the activity model, the standard states and the Henry treatment
      TOGETHER.  This is the one-knob rule, and it is why there is no
      per-component reference-state field anywhere in this case.

  Classification (from canonical component FACTS, never from names):
      water        declared aqueous solvent      (a_w-based VLE)
      CO2          molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation carbonate)
      NH3          molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation ammonia)
      H2S          molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation sulfide)
      aceticAcid   molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation aceticAcid)
      N2           molecular condensable,        (aqueousSpeciation none)
                   nonionising -- soluble, never ionising
      ethanol      molecular condensable,        (aqueousSpeciation none)
                   nonionising
      benzene      molecular condensable,        (aqueousSpeciation none)
                   nonionising
      CaCO3        nonvolatile solid             (calcite / aragonite)
      NH4HCO3      nonvolatile solid             (REFUSES -- see its record)

  MASTERS.  Five, one per family the feed reaches: Acetate (acetic), HCO3
  (carbonate), HS (sulfide), NH4 (ammonia), Ca (calcium).  Each is the
  MAJORITY species of its family at the pH this case reaches -- a master that
  is a vanishing minority (CO3-2, NH3(aq)) conditions the solve badly.  H+
  is shared by every family and is what couples them: this is where the
  acid-base competition between the ammonia and the two acids emerges,
  without any reaction being written for it.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

//  NH4HCO3 is NOT here, and the basis-rank test is what said so.  Its column
//  in the component -> master map is exactly NH3's column plus CO2's
//  (NH4+ and HCO3-, both already carried), so a basis containing all three
//  has a degree of freedom: once dissolved, one mole of ammonium bicarbonate
//  and one mole each of ammonia and carbon dioxide are the SAME species
//  state, and no report could say which the student meant.  The engine
//  refused it by name, with that arithmetic, before this line was written.
//
//  Which is the right answer, and the record's own words say why: the solid
//  "is NEVER FED -- it APPEARS if the NH3 + CO2 loading crosses saturation".
//  A phase that appears is an ADMITTED SOLID (constant/chemistryDict), not a
//  fed component.  The phase set is a RESULT, not an input.
components    ( CO2  CaCO3  H2S  N2  NH3  aceticAcid  benzene  ethanol  water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation electrolyteGammaPhi;

    aqueous
    {
        // composite mixed-solvent electrolyte v1: Davies ions (water-referenced
        // molality) + the molecular backbone on UNIFAC -- a COMPOSITE with
        // proven limits, not an integrated Gibbs surface: no ion-molecule
        // cross terms, K's water-referenced (the mixed-solvent transfer term
        // is a NAMED gap, docs/design/standard-state-transfer-adr.md).
        //
        // UNIFAC, not NRTL, and the reason is the SECOND LIQUID.  A regressed
        // pair is better physics wherever one exists -- and for water-benzene
        // one does not, in this catalogue or in any honest reading of the
        // system: there is no miscible mixture to regress.  Group contribution
        // PREDICTS the immiscibility that a missing pair would silently price
        // as ideal, and a silently ideal water-benzene pair is precisely the
        // wrong answer this case exists to refuse.  It is an ESTIMATE, and the
        // run says so on every line (Vitor, 2026-07-27).
        activityModel { ionic davies;  molecular UNIFAC; }
    }

    //  THE SECOND LIQUID.  20 kmol/h of benzene against 60 of water: they do
    //  not mix, and this case exists to make the engine say so.
    //
    //  Declaring it is not optional bookkeeping.  WITHOUT this block the
    //  system has one liquid, and that liquid would swallow the benzene as
    //  though water and benzene were miscible -- a silently wrong answer the
    //  moment the case's remaining data debt is paid.
    //
    //  The SOLVER now carries this phase (2026-07-27): the split resolves
    //  inside each residual evaluation of the outer vapour Newton by an
    //  equality of activity, and phase appearance/disappearance is decided
    //  outside it and re-tested at the answer.  See
    //  tutorials/steady/flash/flash17_two_liquids_reactive, which is the
    //  same physics on four components and passes.  What still stops THIS
    //  case is its own data debt, listed at the foot of this file.
    //
    //  members is EXPLICIT.  What a phase admits is a modelling decision the
    //  author owns, and the exclusion that matters is not the ~0.2 mol% of
    //  water benzene really holds -- it is that the water-benzene
    //  IMMISCIBILITY which CAUSES the split is declared here rather than
    //  computed, so ethanol's partition rests on two binaries with no ternary
    //  term.  Stated in `reason`, announced on every run.
    organic
    {
        solvent  benzene;
        members  ( benzene  ethanol );
        activityModel UNIFAC;        // must MATCH the aqueous backbone: the
                                     // coupling is an equality of ACTIVITY
        reason
            "pedagogical organic phase: only ethanol is admitted beside the
             benzene.  The immiscibility that causes the split is declared,
             not computed, and no ternary parameter prices it.";
    }

    vapour    { fugacityModel idealGas; }

    volatiles ( CO2  H2S  N2  NH3  aceticAcid  benzene  ethanol  water );
}

/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  WHERE THIS CASE STOPS, and why that is the right place.

  The grammar above is COMPLETE and the SOLVER now serves it: chemistry and
  two liquids at once, which is the fork this case exists to close.  That
  landed on 2026-07-27 and it is demonstrated, passing, by
  tutorials/steady/flash/flash17_two_liquids_reactive -- vapour + a speciated
  aqueous liquid + a benzene-rich organic liquid, the same physics on four
  components.

  An earlier version of this note said a second liquid "has no slot in this
  grammar".  That was wrong twice over.  It is wrong now, because the slot is
  twenty lines above.  And it was wrong then, because the engine has resolved
  vapour + two liquids by direct Gibbs minimisation for a long time
  (IsothermalFlash's PhaseSet::VLLE, with a passing tutorial) -- what the
  engine had never done is BOTH at once.

  WHAT WAS CLEARED on 2026-07-27, and what is left.

  CLEARED, each by the engine naming its own objection:

    1. UNIFAC on the backbone (Vitor's call).  Three NRTL pairs the backbone
       needs do not exist in the public catalogue, and for water-benzene none
       ever will honestly: there is no miscible mixture to regress.  Group
       contribution PREDICTS the immiscibility a missing pair would silently
       price as ideal.  Backbone: UNIFAC (water benzene ethanol).
    2. NH4HCO3 is out of `components`, and the BASIS-RANK TEST is what said
       so -- its column is exactly NH3's plus CO2's, so the basis carried a
       degree of freedom and the engine refused it before anyone reasoned
       about it.  A solid that is never fed is an admitted solid phase, not a
       fed component.  It is not admitted either, because its equilibrium is
       not curated: see NH4HCO3.dat and constant/chemistryDict.
    3. NH4HCO3 also defaulted to `role volatile` and stopped the case on a
       MISSING VAPOUR PRESSURE -- a generic complaint masking the refusal the
       record exists to raise.  Now `role nonvolatile;`.
    4. N2 was being priced on the RAOULT BACKBONE: an Antoine correlation
       with Trange (50, 126) evaluated at 308 K, 180 degrees above nitrogen's
       critical temperature -- a partial pressure read off a curve a long way
       past where it means anything.  A permanent gas has no pure liquid to
       reference.  The engine now REFUSES it by name instead.

  WHAT IS LEFT is ONE NAMED SLICE: the Henry rung for a nonionising dissolved
  gas inside the reactive shape.  The builder already refuses a `role solute`
  component for exactly this reason; nitrogen reaches the same wall by a
  different fact (`noncondensable true`).  What is missing is small and
  specific -- the gas's DISSOLVED amount must enter the speciation as a total,
  and the only record naming that species is its own gas-liquid record
  (`dissolvedSpecies N2`), which the solver reads and the builder does not.

  Writing that bridge a second time on the component looks tempting and is
  wrong: it is a second home for one fact, and check_resolver_coherence says
  so in as many words -- a bridge IS participation, so `aqueousSpeciation
  none;` would then be lying.  It was tried on 2026-07-27 and the gate caught
  it the same hour.

  Everything downstream of that wall was reached and works: with N2 taken out
  of the basis the case assembles completely, masters derive (Acetate Ca HCO3
  HS NH4), the basis is full rank, the second liquid is PRESENT at 95.1 %
  benzene, calcite precipitates 139.8 mg/kg to SI = 0.000 and the pH solves at
  6.78.  The outer flash Newton then still does not converge on this feed --
  a nearly-insoluble inert beside a strongly coupled weak-electrolyte system
  is a harder flash than the seed was built for (flash17 has no inert).  Two
  named slices, then, and both are solver work, not data.

  THE APPROXIMATION, now that the solver has landed.  What is excluded from the
  organic phase is not the ~0.2 mol% of water benzene really holds -- that is
  the small one.  The large one is that the water-benzene IMMISCIBILITY which
  CAUSES the split is declared here rather than computed, so ethanol's
  partition rests on two independent binaries (ethanol-water,
  ethanol-benzene) with no ternary term.  A tie-line from that is an
  arithmetic coincidence between two pairs, not a tie-line of the ternary.
  And ethanol is a co-solvent: above ~40 mol% it closes the miscibility gap
  altogether.  At the ~9 mol% of this feed two liquids still exist, but the
  model does not know why.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
